## ABSTRACT
Carbon-supported transition metal single atoms are promising oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) electrocatalyst. Since there are many types of carbon supports and transition metals, the accurate prediction of the components with high activity through theoretical calculations can greatly save experimental time and costs. In this work, the ORR catalytic properties of 180 types single-atom catalysts (SACs) composed of the eight representative carbon-based substrates (graphdiyne, \( \mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{N} \), \( \mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4} \), phthalocyanine, C-coordination graphene, N-coordination graphene, covalent organic frameworks and metal-organic frameworks) and 3d, 4d, and 5d transition metal elements are investigated by density functional theory (DFT). The adsorption free energy of \( \mathsf{O H^{\star}} \) is proved a universal descriptor capable of accurately prediction of the ORR catalytic activity. It is found that the oxygen reduction reaction overpotentials of all the researched SACs follow one volcano shape very well with the adsorption free energy of \( \mathsf{O H^{\star}} \). Phthalocyanine, N-coordination graphene and metal-organic frameworks stand out as the promising supports for single metal atom due to the relatively lower overpotentials. Notably, the Co-doped metal-organic frameworks, Ir-doped phthalocyanine, Co-doped N-coordination graphene, Co-doped graphdiyne and Rh-doped phthalocyanine show extremely low overpotentials comparable to that of Pt (111). The study provides a guideline for design and selection of carbon-supported SACs toward oxygen reduction reaction.
## KEYWORDS
oxygen reduction reaction, carbon-supported catalysts, high throughput screening, density functional theory, single-atom catalysts
## 1 Introduction
As the crucial reaction for energy conversion in fuel cells and metal-air batteries, oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) determines the overall energy converting efficiency [1, 2]. Great efforts have been dedicated to seek the efficient and long life electrocatalysts [3–5]. So far, Pt-based nanomaterials are commonly utilized as the ORR catalysts due to their outstanding performance. However, the limited source and high cost of Pt cannot yet fulfill the large-scale application demands [6–9]. Fortunately, a large number of low-cost single-atom catalysts (SACs) are found efficient to ORR [10–13]. Since the first SAC of \( \mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{FeO}_{x} \) was reported in 2011 [14], SACs have attracted considerable attention due to their 100% atomic efficiency, highly tunable electronic structure, excellent catalytic efficiency and high selectivity [15–22]. Due to the atomically defined simple structure of the active site, the ORR activity of the SAC can be accurately predicted by the theoretical calculation [23–26].
As the bridge of the theoretical study and the design of heterogenous catalysts, the electronic metal-support interaction has attracted extensive attentions. Regulating atomic configuration can modulate the electronic density of single-atom Co active sites in the metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)-derived Co catalyst polyhedron \( \mathrm{(Co_{1}{-}N_{3}P S/C)} \) [27]. It is found that the support plays a crucial role in regulating the electronic structure of central metal atom. Subsequently, the catalysts with the long-term stability and high catalytic activity are obtained by changing the coordination environment [28–32]. Recently, carbon-based nanomaterials have been proved to be ideal supports of SACs toward ORR owning to their high conductivity, large specific surface area, high corrosion resistance and low cost [33–41]. For instance, Xu et al. [42] calculated the ORR, oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and hydrogen evolution reactions (HER) performance on C/N-doped graphene with a single transition metal (TM) atom. They reported that the catalytic activity of SACs was closely correlated with the local environment of the metal center, and a series of SACs to catalyze ORR, OER and HER have been found out. Li et al. [43] considered the reaction free energy and energy barrier for ORR on N-coordination graphene catalysts embedded TM atom, in which Mn atom is proved the optimal metal active site. The report about ORR on high-valent metal complexes of g-C3N4 (TM-OH-C3N4) indicates that the additional OH group decreases the adsorption energies of ORR intermediates, thus the overpotentials of ORR on Ni/Cu-OH-C3N4 are much close to that on Pt (111) [44]. For single TM atom (from Sc to Zn and Pt) embedded in graphdiyne (GDY) nanosheet, the Ni-GDY composite possesses the lowest overpotential of 0.29 and 0.40 V for OER and ORR, respectively
