[35]. However, these reports are focused only on the effects of various metal active centers on catalytic active of the SAC with a single substrate, and the influence of supports on catalytic active was ignored. The comparison in catalytic performance among different types of carbon-based substrates is rare.
To select the optimal ORR catalyst from an enormous number of candidates, high-throughput screening method is widely used [45, 46]. For example, to acquire the high-performance ORR catalysts of two-dimensional double-metal sulfide \(\left(\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\right)\), screening almost all TM candidates [47]. The ORR activity screening of Pt, \(\mathrm{M}\) alloys in 21 TM atoms have been evaluated [48]. Nevertheless, due to the high cost and the low efficient, it is not practical to utilize high-throughput computational screening for a large number of possible TM and carbon-supports combinations for multi-step reactions. The introduction of activity descriptors can speed up the screening process and reduce the screening cost. To date, the adsorption free energies of reaction intermediates, d-band center of metal atom, the number of d orbital electron, and the electronegativity of TM are considered to be the descriptors of ORR activity. Notably, \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{OH}^{+}}\) as a descriptor for ORR activities shows the great accuracy and universality on carbon, transition metal sulfide and MXenes supported SACs [20, 24, 35, 39, 47, 49–51]. However, the descriptor was only applied to describe a particular support in above studies. A uniform rule has not been established among different supports.
In this work, we first constructed 48 SACs, which consist of six metal atoms (Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, and Cu) paired with eight carbon supports (GDY, \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{N}\), \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\), phthalocyanine (Pc), C-coordination graphene, N-coordination graphene, covalent organic frameworks (COF) and metal-organic frameworks (MOF)). Based on density functional theory calculation, the adsorption and catalytic activity of these SACs towards ORR are systematic investigated. It is found that the adsorption free energy of \(\mathrm{OH^{+}}\) can accurately predict the overpotentials of all carbon-supported SACs, and a volcanic relationship is established for the \(\mathrm{OH^{+}}\) adsorption and catalytic activity. Furthermore, this relationship can be extended to the 3d, 4d, and 5d TM atoms. In addition, the stability of the SACs with high ORR activities was further assessed by the difference of binding energies and cohesive energies.
## 2 Computational models and details
Figure 1 shows the models of eight types of carbon-based substrates (GDY, \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{N}\), \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\), Pc, C-coordination graphene, N-coordination graphene, COF, and MOF) used in this study. Six TM atoms (Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, and Cu) are embedded in these substrates one by one, and a total of 48 SACs are constructed. Among them, GDY is a two-dimensional periodic carbon allotrope containing sp- and \(\mathrm{sp}^{2}\)-hybridized carbon atoms. For \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{N}\), the unique porous structure is originated from two-dimensional (2D) graphene layer and provides desired support for metallic active center. When TM atom is doped in Pc substrate (TM-Pc), each phthalocyanine function binds to a TM atom through its four inwardly projecting N atoms, forming four TM–N bonds. The TM atom doped in graphene directly or co-doped with N atom on the graphene support were denoted as TM-C and TM-N-C, respectively. For COF, the terpyridine structure was chosen, in which an embedded TM atom binds to three N coordination atoms [52]. In TM-MOF, a periodic distribution of TM atom overcomes the difficulties of immobilization and greatly enhancing the catalytic performance of MOF-supported SACs [53, 54].
For most SACs, the substrates prefer to keep the planer structure without distinct upshift after the structure optimization.
All the structure optimization were performed using the plane-wave density functional theory approach as implemented in the Vienna ab-initio simulation package (VASP) code [60, 61]. Generalized gradient approximation (GGA) in conjunction with the Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) functional was employed to describe the exchange-correlation interactions [62, 63]. The projector augmented wave (PAW) pseudo-potentials was used to represent ionic potentials [64]. And the energy cutoff of \(500\,\mathrm{eV}\) was chosen for the plane wave basis. All the SACs slab models were constructed by consulting previous reports [33, 42, 53, 65–67], and a vacuum layer of \(20\,\mathrm{\AA}\) was adopted to avoid periodic interaction. For geometry optimization of hexagonal and rectangular supercell, the Brillouin zone integration was sampled by \(2 \times 2 \times 1\) and \(4 \times 2 \times 1\) Monkhorst–Pack meshes, respectively [68]. During the geometry relaxation, the shape and size of the supercell, as well as the atomic positions were fully relaxed by the conjugate gradient method. And the convergence criterion of each atom for the force and energy were set to \(0.01\,\mathrm{eV}.\mathrm{\AA}^{-1}\) and \(10^{-5}\,\mathrm{eV}\). The long-range van der Waals (vdW) interactions were described by the \(\mathrm{DFT} + \mathrm{D3}\) method [69].
For ORR process, we only consider the four-electron reaction pathway in acidic media \((\mathrm{pH} = 0)\) [36]:
\[
\mathrm{O}_2(\mathrm{g})+\mathrm{e}^{-}+\mathrm{H}^{+}\longrightarrow\mathrm{OCH}^{+}
\]
\[
\mathrm{OOH^{*}+e^{-}+H^{+}\rightarrow H_{2}O(l)+O^{*}}
\]
\[
\mathrm{O^{*}+e^{-}+H^{+}\rightarrow OH^{*}}
\]
\[
\mathrm{OH^{*}+e^{-}+H^{+}\rightarrow H_{2}O(l)+^{*}}
\]
The overall ORR could be described by the following equation:
\[
\mathrm{O}_2(\mathbf{g})+4\mathbf{e}^-+4\mathrm{H}^+\longrightarrow2\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{l})
\]
