## 3.3 Stability
We finally evaluate the catalyst stability. The binding energy \((E_{\mathrm{binding}})\) of TM atom on supports and TM bulk cohesive energy \((E_{\mathrm{coh}})\) have been calculated by:
\[
E_{\mathrm{binding}}=E_{\mathrm{TM/support}}-E_{\mathrm{support}}-E_{\mathrm{TM}}
\]
\[
E_{\mathrm{coh}}=E_{\mathrm{TM/bulk}}/N-E_{\mathrm{TM}}
\]
where \(E_{\mathrm{TM/support}}\,,\,E_{\mathrm{support}}\) and \(E_{\mathrm{TM}}\) are the total energies of the substrate with and without TM atom, and the single TM atom in vacuum. \(E_{\mathrm{TM/bulk}}\) and \(N\) denote the total energy and the number of atoms for TM bulk unit cell, respectively.
The calculated results are shown in Table 1. The binding energies for all the systems are negative from \(-12.11\) to \(-2.24\ \mathrm{eV}\). The strong binding strength can prevent the migration of single TM atoms on the support. It can be found that a weaker binding strength between single metal atom and support in \(\mathrm{Au-C}_{2}\mathrm{N}\) catalysts corresponding to a longer average distance between TM atom and coordination atoms \((2.52\mathrm{~\AA~})\). On the contrast, the Ir-Pc model processes the strongest binding of \(-12.11\ \mathrm{eV}\) with a short \(d_{\mathrm{ave}}\) of \(1.97\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\), and the calculated \(\Delta E_{\mathrm{binding}}\,-\,\Delta E_{\mathrm{coh}}\) is negative, which can avoid TM atoms clustering on the surface. Similarly, the values of \(\Delta E_{\mathrm{binding}}\) – \(\Delta E_{\mathrm{coh}}\) for Co-N-C, Co-GDY and Rh-Pc are \(-3.18\), \(-0.12\), and \(-4.97\ \mathrm{eV}\), respectively, indicating the aggregation of TM atoms on the catalyst surface is unfavorable. Previous reports demonstrate that the Co-MOF catalyst exhibit a good thermally stability [72]. It can be expected that Co-MOF, Ir–Pc, Co–N–C, Co-GDY, and Rh–Pc would exhibit high stability in practical application.
## 4 Conclusion
In conclusion, the catalytic activities for ORR of 48 SACs consisting of six metal atoms and eight carbon-supported have been evaluated by means of density functional theory. We proposed that using descriptor \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{OH}^{+}}\) can realize a fast-scan/accurate prediction for SACs on diverse carbon-supported. Then the \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{OH}^{+}}\) for 3d, 4d, and 5d TM atoms doped in eight carbon-based materials have been calculated to predict the ORR overpotential on these SACs. The comparison between PDS derived overpotentials and \(\Delta G_{\mathrm{OH}^{+}}\) derived overpotentials on studied SACs manifests that this method has a high forecast precision. It is predicted that Co-MOF, Ir-Pc, Co-N-C, Co-GDY, and Rh-Pc possess overpotential around \(0.35\ \mathrm{~V}\), superior than that of Pt catalyst. Moreover, these catalysts exhibit a high stability, ensuring the great promise for experimental synthesis. This study not only provides massive information to guide the experimental designs of carbon-supported TM SACs, but also provides a reliable prediction method to evaluate the ORR activity.
